{
  "meta": {
    "today": "2026-09-07",
    "weekday": "Monday",
    "version": "time-de-halb-1.0",
    "note": "The productivity contrast inside German. The earlier German probe varied productivity across two languages; this varies it inside one. halb acht (07:30) is a stored counting-into phrase; zehn vor halb acht (07:20) is counting into the hour assembled from that phrase, with the surface number not the answer and the named hour not yet arrived, which is the profile that breaks in Russian; zwanzig nach sieben (07:20) counts from the completed hour. The from_hour and into_composed cells ask for the SAME eight clock times with BYTE-IDENTICAL English, since English has only one rendering of 06:20, so a difference between them cannot be the task or the time and can only be the German construction. This file exists because the paper claimed German has no productive counting-into form, which is false; see F70. Daypart words avoid the 02:00-05:00 window that an earlier review found mislabelled as morgens, and vormittags is not used at all. The post-positioned daypart is kept despite being an English calque, because every earlier German probe uses it and changing it here would make the files incomparable. PREDICTIONS REGISTERED IN NOTES F71 BEFORE THE RUN. WORDING NOT CHECKED BY A NATIVE SPEAKER."
  },
